  "behavioral": {
    "values": [
      "Craft and attention to detail",
      "Collaboration across disciplines",
      "Working with ambiguity in long cycles"
    ],
    "stories": [
      {
        "story": "Making the agent something other teams could actually use",
        "prompt": "Tell me about something you built for other engineers rather than for users.",
        "situation": "Aleph Lab had a production AI agent that only worked inside our own application. Other teams wanted it, but there was no way to embed it.",
        "task": "Turn it into something another team could drop into their app without needing to understand our internals.",
        "action": "I packaged it as a typed SDK with versioned APIs and wrote 136 automated tests. I treated the test suite as the contract: if the package was genuinely self-contained, someone with no context should be able to integrate it from the types alone.",
        "result": "A new AI coding agent integrated it into a fresh app on the first attempt — the clearest evidence the abstraction actually held, since it had no context to fall back on.",
        "mapsTo": "Collaboration across disciplines"
      },
      {
        "story": "Owning one layer of a three-person engine",
        "prompt": "Tell me about a technical project you're proud of.",
        "situation": "Mini Minecraft was a from-scratch voxel engine in C++ and OpenGL built by a team of three. I owned world generation and the render pipeline; chunk streaming and player physics belonged to my teammates.",
        "task": "Build a world that read as varied rather than noisy, and a renderer that made it legible.",
        "action": "I built 7-biome procedural generation with Perlin, FBM and Voronoi noise using smoothstep blending, carved 3D Perlin-noise caves, and wrote the GLSL render pipeline — PCF shadow mapping, screen-space reflections, vertex ambient occlusion, a day-night cycle, and post-process water and lava overlays through a framebuffer pass.",
        "result": "A coherent world where generation and lighting reinforced each other. Building inside someone else's streaming system also forced my generation to be correct at chunk boundaries, not just in isolation.",
        "mapsTo": "Craft and attention to detail"
      },
      {
        "story": "The outage nobody had noticed",
        "prompt": "Walk me through the hardest bug you've debugged. How did you find it?",
        "situation": "Aleph Lab's notification pipeline was failing silently in production. Nothing alerted, and the failure was invisible from inside the product.",
        "task": "Find out why notifications were not being delivered, and restore the pipeline.",
        "action": "I traced backwards from the delivery failures rather than guessing at causes, and found a JWT signing-key migration: the pipeline was still authenticating with a key that was no longer valid, so every request was being rejected. The migration itself was correct — nothing downstream had been updated to follow it.",
        "result": "The pipeline went from failing with 401 to healthy at 200. The instructive part was that the symptom (no notifications) and the cause (an auth key rotation) lived in completely different parts of the system.",
        "mapsTo": "Working with ambiguity in long cycles"
      },
      {
        "story": "Failing an intro CS course, and what actually caused it",
        "prompt": "Tell me about your biggest failure. (Palantir's hiring-manager round asks this directly.)",
        "situation": "Early in my time at Penn I overloaded a semester and failed an introductory CIS course — the foundational one for everything I've built since.",
        "task": "The honest read isn't that the material was too hard. I had taken on more than I could carry, and rather than triage early I let one course slide and told myself I'd recover it at the end. I couldn't.",
        "action": "I then served two years as a military interpreter, which was two years of work where falling behind quietly was not an option. When I came back to Penn I retook the course and changed the thing that had actually gone wrong: I kept pace week to week instead of banking on a recovery later, and treated falling behind as something to raise immediately rather than absorb.",
        "result": "I retook it and earned an A−, and went on to the graphics and systems coursework that the whole rest of my work depends on. The durable lesson wasn't 'work harder' — it was that problems compound silently while you're deciding not to look at them, which is the same instinct behind checking the retention targeting and reconstructing that retention number when nobody had asked me to.",
        "mapsTo": "Working with ambiguity in long cycles"
      }
    ]
  },
  "askThem": [
    "Which team is this, and what does it own end to end?",
    "How much does an intern interact with Human Interface designers on this team?",
    "What does the long product cycle mean for what an intern actually ships?"
  ]
}
